# Source encoding - UTF-8 only (no UTF-16)

On Windows, Cursor **Write** / **StrReplace** can save text as **UTF-16 LE**. That breaks `dotnet build` (`CS1056 Unexpected character '\0'`), PowerShell parse, and SQL scripts.

## Never do

- Do **not** save `.cs`, `.ps1`, `.sql`, `.json`, `.md`, `.yaml`, `.xafml`, or other repo text as UTF-16.
- Do **not** "fix" encoding by re-writing with tools that may emit UTF-16 again (`Out-File` default, `Set-Content` without `-Encoding utf8NoBOM`, `Write`/`StrReplace` in a loop without verifying bytes).
- Do **not** treat `\0` / mojibake on line 1 as a real syntax bug - check encoding first.

## Always do

- Prefer **UTF-8 without BOM** for all text sources in this repo.
- After creating or heavily editing a file with Cursor tools on Windows, if build/parse fails oddly, **verify encoding** before debugging logic:

```powershell
# First bytes of a .cs file should be "using" -> 117,115,105,110 (not 255,254 / 0 between chars)
([System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes($path)[0..7] | ForEach-Object { $_.ToString('X2') }) -join ' '
```

## Converting UTF-16-corrupted files back to UTF-8

When a file is already UTF-16 (BOM `FF FE`, or every other byte `00`):

```powershell
$path = 'C:\full\path\to\File.cs'
$text = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($path)   # .NET detects UTF-16 via BOM
$utf8 = New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding $false
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $text, $utf8)
```

- Use **`[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText(..., UTF8Encoding($false))`** - not `Out-File`, not `Set-Content` without explicit UTF-8 no BOM.
- Re-read bytes: `.cs` starting with `using` must begin `75 73 69 6E` (hex), not `FF FE` or `75 00 73 00`.
- Prefer one PowerShell rewrite over repeated Cursor `Write` on the same corrupted path.

## New files when tools keep corrupting encoding

Write content via PowerShell UTF-8 no BOM instead of relying on Cursor `Write` for that file:

```powershell
$utf8 = New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding $false
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $content, $utf8)
```